Local callback writers: messaging registry and host mktty
========================================================

Run from the repository root (no kernel build):

  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/messaging_registry_test.sh
  sh tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/mktty_lifetime_test.sh

Both scripts extract the current production functions verbatim into a temporary
header, compile a host harness, and remove their temporary directory on exit.
CC and CFLAGS select the compiler/options; for example repeat both commands with
CC=gcc CFLAGS=-O2, then CC=clang
CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-omit-frame-pointer'.
Assertions must remain enabled. mktty's harness suppresses unused-parameter and
sign-compare warnings present in the extracted baseline file operations.

Messaging registry
------------------

The mutex serializes duplicate check, lower-level callback registration and list
insertion. Unregister holds it through lower-level callback retirement and context
freeing, so a replacement of the same type cannot overlap the old callback's
retirement. Cleanup closes admission before detaching the list and retains the
mutex until every detached handler is retired. A concurrent cleanup therefore
cannot report completion while another cleanup still drains callbacks.

Admission begins closed. Init opens it once; cleanup permanently closes it even
if called before init. Registration while closed returns -ESHUTDOWN; another init
returns -EALREADY. Allocation failure and duplicate/missing-handler errors leave
the mutex usable. No message layout, peer/session protocol or API version changes.

Register, unregister and cleanup require sleepable process context and no
callback-owned locks. Interrupt-context attempts are warned and rejected
(-EWOULDBLOCK for register/unregister; cleanup returns without changing state).
Other atomic process-context callers remain forbidden by the mutex contract.
The callback accesses its immutable per-type context, never the registry mutex;
callbacks must not register/unregister or clean up messaging. Owners retain their
callback context until successful unregister or completed cleanup. Production
multikernel_unregister_handler() supplies the existing RCU grace-period guarantee.

Seven controlled scenarios exercise competing same-type writers, unregister vs
replacement, cleanup vs registration, concurrent cleanups, closed admission and
one-shot init, allocation/error paths, and interrupt-context rejection. An entered
callback runs the extracted message wrapper while a remover waits at the modeled
RCU boundary; its context must not be freed before callback return. Checkpoints
use condition variables and observed lock contention, not scheduling sleeps.

Host mktty
----------

Open/release now use IRQ-save locking of the same connection-list lock held by
the hardIRQ receive callback. This excludes same-CPU interrupt reentry. The
callback retains the list lock through RX use and wakeup; release unlinks under
that lock before freeing the RX buffer and connection. Concurrent file operations
continue to rely on VFS file references delaying final .release().

First writes validate the instance outside the spinlock, then publish the target
once under the connection-list lock with WRITE_ONCE. A competing first write
that also observed an unconnected FD returns -EISCONN after losing publication.
Later read/poll/write paths use READ_ONCE; a data write snapshots the immutable
target once for all chunks. Callback lookup reads the target under the publication
lock. This does not pin a remote instance for the connection's entire lifetime or
solve remote restart/ID reuse; each send retains its existing lookup behavior.

Four scenarios check IRQ exclusion at open/release lock-acquisition checkpoints,
release waiting for a callback paused during wakeup, simultaneous first-target
writers plus read/poll/data writes, and failed target validation/nonblocking I/O.
The IRQ checkpoint records the baseline's potential reentrant deadlock rather
than deliberately hanging a host thread. Baseline cross-thread callback/release
lifetime and validation scenarios already passed; their lifetime scheme remains.

Evidence and limits
-------------------

Tests were written and run before production changes at c5f7c18e1229:
messaging's initial six scenarios failed (duplicate successes, retirement
overlap, surviving post-cleanup registration, open-before-init/reopen, missing
context constraint); mktty failed two of four (IRQ exclusion and competing target
publication/marked reads). Concurrent-cleanup and broader context checks were
then added to messaging's suite.

Final checks: all seven messaging and four mktty scenarios passed with plain cc,
GCC -O2 and Clang -O1 ASan+UBSan (including alignment). The existing extracted
ipi_lifetime_test.sh passed with plain cc, and git diff --check passed. No kernel
build was run.

These are production-extraction behavioral tests with NON-NATIVE boundaries.
pthread mutexes stand in for kernel locks; registry callback registration/RCU
retirement, local IRQ masking, scheduler, allocation and VFS/instance operations
are controlled substitutes. They are not a native kernel, lockdep, KCSAN, physical
IPI, RCU or hardware qualification. The existing ipi_lifetime_test.sh separately
extracts the lower-level registration/removal/dispatch path.

Remaining gates: native non-RT IRQ/lockdep and callback-drain stress on controller
and worker paths; actual multikernel/TTY use and remote retirement/restart evidence.
PREEMPT_RT remains explicitly UNQUALIFIED: ordinary spinlocks in callback pending,
mktty RX and wait/wakeup paths remain baseline limitations. Host compiler/sanitizer
success must not be interpreted as full RT safety, complete driver/DMA safety,
foreign-writer retirement or a wire/session lifetime protocol.
